      'raw': dict({
        'cook_time': None,
        'directions': list([
          'For the colocasium (which is really the colocasia plant, also called “Egyptian bean”) use pepper, cumin, rue, honey, or broth, and a little oil.',
          'When done bind with roux.',
        ]),
        'ingredients': list([
          'Colocasium (root of the Egyptian bean)',
          'Pepper',
          'Cumin',
          'Rue',
          'Honey',
          'Broth',
          'A little oil',
          'Roux',
        ]),
        'name': 'COLOCASIUM TARO, DASHEEN',
        'notes': 'Colocasium is the root of the Egyptian bean which is used exclusively. The root or tubers of this plant was used by the ancients as a vegetable. They probably boiled and then peeled and sliced the tubers, seasoning the pieces with the above ingredients, heated them in bouillon stock and thickened the gravy in the usual way. Since the tuber is very starchy, little roux is required for binding. The U.S. Department of Agriculture has in recent years imported various specimens of that taro species (belonging to the colocasia), and the plants are now successfully being farmed in the southern parts of the United States, with fair prospects of becoming an important article of daily diet. It can be prepared in every way like a potato, and possesses advantages over the potato as far as value of nutrition, flavor, culture and keeping qualities are concerned.',
