          'A PINT OF BROTH',
          '1 SEXTARIUS OF OIL',
          '1 PINT OF WATER',
        ]),
        'name': 'PICKLED SUCKLING PIG',
        'notes': 'exodionum, and in the Summary of Dishes, exozome, i.e. oxyzomum. It is curious to note the various spellings and meanings of oxyzomum. This is supposed to be a sour sauce or an acid preparation of some kind, yet this recipe does not mention acids. In fact, the presence of honey would make it a sweet preparation. We take it, the “garnish” contains the necessary vinegar or other acids such as lemon juice, wine, etc. Oxyzomum is properly rendered “pickle.” Dann. oil, occurring twice in his version. sæpius; Dann. confusing sæpe with cæpa, renders this “onions sauce.” The same occurs to him in XXVII.',
